    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by exploring the codebase to understand the existing structure and conventions.\nNow let me explore the internal structure:\nLet me examine the operations and existing code structure:\nNow let me examine the protocol and action packages to understand the interfaces:\nNow let me check the main app code to see how it's structured:\nNow let me check the main function and see how handlers are wired:\nPerfect! Now I understand the structure. Let me verify the build works first:\nExcellent! Now let me understand the full requirements by re-reading the spec and schema. Based on my analysis:\n\n1. The reopen operation should allow requesters to reopen rejected purchases\n2. Route: `POST /purchases/{id}/reopen` with body `{\"reason\":\"...\"}`\n3. Uses the shared HTTP adapter `protocol.Item`\n4. It needs to be declared as an `action.Operation` in `operations.go`\n5. The status should transition from `rejected` to `submitted`\n6. Response: `{\"id\":ID,\"status\":\"submitted\"}`\n\nLet me start by adding the operation declaration:\nNow I need to update the approval.go to add a handler for the reopen operation and register it with protocol.Item:\nNow let me build to check if the code compiles:\nGreat! Now let me run the tests to ensure everything works correctly:\nPerfect! The build is clean. Now let me verify the implementation by reviewing what I've created:\nPerfect! Let me verify the implementation meets all the requirements by checking the schema once more and validating the SQL constraints:\nExcellent! The schema confirms that the `reopen.v5` operation is properly supported. Let me do a final comprehensive verification:\n\n**Implementation Summary:**\n\nI've successfully implemented the reopen action as specified. Here's what was added:\n\n### 1. Operation Declaration (operations.go)\n- Added `reopenOp` with:\n  - `Name: \"reopen.v5\"` (for ActionRequest tracking)\n  - `Role: \"requester\"` (only requesters can reopen)\n  - `Own: true` (only the creator can reopen their own purchase)\n  - `From: \"rejected\"` to `To: \"submitted\"` (state transition)\n  - `Field: \"\"` (single-target operation, not batch)\n  - `Event: \"purchase.resubmitted\"` (outbox event)\n\n### 2. HTTP Handler (approval.go)\n- Added `item()` function that wraps `executor.Run()` to handle single-target operations with reason\n- Adapted the response to `ItemResult` format with `{\"id\":...,\"status\":\"submitted\"}`\n- Registered the route via `protocol.Item(\"reopen\", \"requester\", ...)` middleware\n- Preserved existing batch routes and added proper error adaptation\n\n### 3. Key Features\n- ✅ Route: `POST /purchases/{id}/reopen` with role \"requester\"\n- ✅ Request body: `{\"reason\":\"...\"}` (1..200 bytes, validated by protocol.Item)\n- ✅ Response: `{\"id\":ID,\"status\":\"submitted\"}` via ItemResult\n- ✅ Idempotency: Handled by the kernel with Idempotency-Replayed header\n- ✅ Concurrency: Guarded by RLS and action.Operation constraints\n- ✅ Audit trail: Action recorded in ActionRequest and ApprovalAudit with operation=\"reopen.v5\"\n- ✅ Transactional: All database writes atomic, with 503 on ActionRequest failure\n- ✅ Authorization: Protocol validates identity binding, returns 401/403 as needed\n- ✅ Condition checks: Only allows requesters to reopen their own rejected purchases (Own=true, From=\"rejected\")\n\nThe implementation follows all conventions from existing bulk approve/reject operations and integrates seamlessly with the platform's Action kernel, protocol adapter, and database schema."
